  "body": "**Incremental review:** [Files changed](https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/VeOmni/pull/1200/files) against `main`.\n\n### What does this PR do?\n\nImplement and validate DeepSeek V4 fullgraph torch.compile training for #992, independently of EP validation in #1199. Both target main independently. Enabled ExtraParallel plus compilation remains rejected.\n\nThe latest precision fix makes batch-invariant Triton GEMM use explicit IEEE input precision. Its default dot mode truncated FP32 projection operands to TF32 even when PyTorch matmul precision was `highest`, while compiled ATen matmul followed a different policy. The fix retains native BF16/FP16 dot behavior and does not claim that production BF16 whole-model parity is solved.\n\n### Design and Code Changes\n\n- Use traceable tensor assertions and compile-safe kernel configuration selection.\n- Skip Python-only pretuning log inspection during graph capture.\n- Use explicit IEEE dot input precision for batch-invariant GEMM.\n- Cover decoder fullgraph forward/backward, Inductor expert VJP, actual packed FSDP2 training execution, and an independent FP64 GEMM oracle with bias, striding, padded shapes and batch partitions.\n\n### Is compiled BF16 less accurate, or just differently rounded?\n\nOne fixed set of BF16-rounded weights, one input, and an eager FP32 forward/backward over those same values as the reference. The numbers below come from the pre-rebase harness; the current `test_deepseek_v4_compiled_decoder_gradients_track_the_fp32_oracle` checks gradient direction (cosine) against this reference, not the relative-L2 values shown. The reference runs the eager MoE implementation, so its distance also contains that implementation difference.\n\n| Mode | Loss | Gradient distance to the FP32 reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| FP32 reference | 6.958946 | — |\n| BF16 eager | 6.959341 | 1.70% |\n| BF16 Inductor, fullgraph | 6.958899 | **1.48%** |\n| BF16 eager vs BF16 Inductor | — | 2.08% |\n\nInductor is if anything closer to the reference, so the eager/Inductor gradient gap is rounding divergence rather than a compiled-path accuracy regression.\n\nFour-rank packed FSDP2 run, eager vs Inductor whole-model pre-clipping gradient relative L2:\n\n| Step | Default | `TORCHINDUCTOR_EMULATE_PRECISION_CASTS=1` |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 5.48% | 5.84% |\n| 2 | 5.79% | 6.02% |\n\n| Supporting statistic (four-rank, step 1) | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Pre-clipping gradient norm difference | 1.5e-4 relative |\n| Cosine between the two gradients | 0.9985 |\n| Expert-parameter share of the squared error | 15.2% |\n| Parameters above 1% relative | 109 of 110 |\n\n### Test\n\nMeasured on the pre-rebase revision (af2a1c2b), before the tests were ported to the VeomniOp registry; not re-run on the current head.\n\n| Suite | Result | Wall time |\n|---|---|---|\n| `make quality` (ruff check + format) | PASS, 610 files | — |\n| `tests/distributed/test_torch_compile.py` + `tests/ops/test_batch_invariant_matmul_precision.py` | 81 passed | 161s |\n| `tests/distributed/test_torch_compile.py -k compiled_decoder_gradients_track_the_fp32_oracle` | 2 passed | 39s |\n| Batch-invariant GEMM cases (FP32/BF16/FP16 × shape × bias × seed) | 36 passed, includes exact batch-partition equality | included above |\n\nCoverage of the compiled path, all asserted rather than reported:\n\n| Test | Assertion |\n|---|---|\n| `test_deepseek_v4_fused_decoder_fullgraph_forward_backward_matches_eager[eager]` | compiled `eager`-backend loss within `rtol=atol=1e-4`, every parameter gradient within `rtol=atol=1e-3` of eager |\n| `test_deepseek_v4_inductor_fullgraph_executes_finite_optimizer_step` | all decoder blocks compiled; two batches, each with finite loss, finite gradients and an AdamW step |\n| `test_deepseek_v4_inductor_fixed_route_expert_vjp_matches_fp32_reference[1,2,3]` | fixed-route expert output within `rtol=1e-2, atol=2e-2` of FP32 math; input/routing/weight gradients with cosine > 0.999 and within `rtol=2e-2, atol=3e-2`; compiled bitwise equal to eager |\n| `test_deepseek_v4_compiled_decoder_gradients_track_the_fp32_oracle[1,2]` | losses within `rtol=5e-3, atol=1e-2` of the FP32 reference; gradient cosine to the reference > 0.995 per mode, modes within 2e-3, per-parameter Inductor > 0.97 and at most 5e-3 below eager (direction only, no magnitude bound) |\n\n### API and CI\n\nNo new public API; configuration and training entry points are unchanged. GPU CI runs `tests/distributed/test_torch_compile.py` and the whole `tests/ops/` directory, so the new cases are picked up with no workflow change.\n\n### Remaining limitations\n\n- Static 512-token shapes, EP/SP off, eager DSA/mHC/norm/shared MLP/loss; the numbers above do not qualify whole-model convergence or long-context training.\n- SM90+ kernels (TileLang DSA/indexer, FA4 + Quack) are not qualified on the SM89 CI fleet.\n- The decoder-level oracle comparison is not a whole-model acceptance test, and the FP32 reference changes the expert implementation as well as its precision.\n- The eager/Inductor gap is reported, not fixed: closing it would require either reproducing eager's per-op rounding under fusion or moving both paths to a shared higher-precision local implementation.\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **Documentation**\n  - Added guidance for validating compiled DeepSeek V4 training, numerical tolerances, expert-kernel checks, and known BF16 limitations.\n  - Documented batch-invariant matrix multiplication precision tests and Triton IEEE input-precision behavior.\n\n- **Bug Fixes**\n  - Improved DeepSeek V4 position handling across supported execution modes.\n  - Improved reliability of compiled operation outputs.\n  - Standardized matrix multiplication input precision for more predictable results.\n\n- **Tests**\n  - Added comprehensive compiled-training, gradient-equivalence, RMSNorm, and batch-invariance coverage across supported CUDA configurations.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n",
